  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "`S1.D6` requires the depth-`T` unrolling to match the original on every finite input list, not just through `T` steps. This is false even for `T = 1`: on `[true, false]`, the supplied system `d15` remembers the `true`, but its one-layer unrolling returns `false`. The claimed horizon-bounded equality is `S1.D5 T (N.d14 T) N`."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "There is no recurrence hypothesis on `N`, so the target also covers already-feedforward systems. P2's recurrent-to-feedforward direction only requires the result for recurrent systems."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "P2 states existence without specifying a horizon or size. The target selects a particular construction for each natural `T`, including zero, with exactly `T` copies of the units. This is a constructive refinement motivated by the timestep-copying passage, not a size requirement in P2 itself."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The formal model specializes the source's general systems to finitely many units with synchronous deterministic updates and a fixed initial state. It tests feedforwardness by acyclicity of functional update dependencies rather than by a separately specified wiring diagram."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The acyclicity and copy-count clauses capture the layered unrolling. The material error is that `T` bounds the number of layers but does not bound the input histories on which the target demands equal outputs."
